{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"bc935c2e2b851a71","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Man’s search for meaning-1782342139101.webp","x":-220,"y":-200,"width":271,"height":432},
		{"id":"b303a64f52b646b6","type":"text","text":"[[Viktor E. Frankl]]","x":220,"y":-340,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a49730883d8dfe3f","type":"text","text":"[[Dehumanization]] - Everybody treated as a nothing","x":320,"y":-14,"width":300,"height":94},
		{"id":"efc391470a3c3c6b","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “Any guard who wanted to make a charge against a prisoner just glanced at his number; he never asked for his name.\n\nPrisoners classified by numbers, not anything else.","x":720,"y":-307,"width":513,"height":215},
		{"id":"c6ea0d01ff0691df","type":"text","text":"All prisoners had their hair removed, tattooed with a number and wore the same clothes. All character of a person was stolen.","x":842,"y":80,"width":391,"height":111},
		{"id":"deae5faedecdb570","type":"text","text":"[[Appreciate what you have right now]]","x":320,"y":400,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d402432dbd23c8f5","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “I became the proud owner of a token worth twelve cigarettes … could be exchanged for 12 soups.\n> \n\n12 watery soups became something to be ecstatic about. This makes me appreciate that I can even eat food at home.\n","x":772,"y":297,"width":648,"height":243},
		{"id":"a73dc8a7516e3afa","type":"text","text":"Upon entering, a left finger → gas, a right finger → work","x":922,"y":-49,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ac8c0acbe427b85d","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “‘Then you can see him there,’ I was told. ‘Where?’ A hand pointed to the chinmey a few hundred yards off, which was sending a column of flame up  into the grey sky of Poland. It dissolved into a sinister cloud of smoke.\n\nHumans → gas chamber → meaningless smoke.\nHow would you feel about witnessing that?","x":1360,"y":-126,"width":700,"height":262},
		{"id":"a70896474d29aff6","type":"text","text":"**Untermenschen**\nNazis classified millions as ‘subhuman’ or ‘life unworthy of living’ ","x":957,"y":-453,"width":430,"height":113},
		{"id":"96bb3114524b7ca6","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “But one thing I beg of you … shave daily … look fit for work.”\n\nIf you didn’t look fit for work, you would be killed immediately","x":-1450,"y":860,"width":458,"height":244},
		{"id":"f11c5a443e6dc6ea","type":"text","text":"**Always smile, nomatter what**","x":-1270,"y":1170,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"42be7c62e7c4d120","type":"text","text":"Constantly have to dodge from being sent to the gas chamber","x":-895,"y":1000,"width":305,"height":90},
		{"id":"25810461eae0aea8","type":"text","text":"**Disease**","x":-759,"y":1200,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"66b08d07551796e5","type":"text","text":"**Daily life**","x":-470,"y":970,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"73cf51c58628eba0","type":"text","text":"Frankl suffered from *edema* (swelling)","x":-1180,"y":1360,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"46afa07c70387537","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “So my partly bear feet were always wet and my shoes were full of snow. This caused frostbite and chillbains. Every step became torture.”\n\n[[Frostbite, edema, every step is torture]]","x":-1800,"y":1278,"width":465,"height":225},
		{"id":"f10d5e84aee1961d","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “This camp had no ‘oven’ no crematorium, no gas!\n> pp.56\n> \n\nFrankl and others were appreciating and happy to go to a camp that we would call inhumane. Shows the drastic shrink of what to appreciate.\n","x":716,"y":595,"width":644,"height":225},
		{"id":"131af744e0ee2732","type":"text","text":"Human life *is so valuable* and we must make the most of not just our lives, but also others.","x":1344,"y":-256,"width":316,"height":96},
		{"id":"f113733675aa302c","type":"text","text":"[[Mortality]]","x":1780,"y":-426,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"27b76db72eaeb7b5","type":"text","text":"**Liberation**","x":320,"y":1032,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a3741a11a7e5dc89","type":"text","text":"> “Our friends who had thought they were travelling to freedom that night had been taken in the trucks to this camp, and there they were locked in the huts and burned to death.”\n> pp.70","x":1180,"y":1004,"width":520,"height":177},
		{"id":"9dd7e2ce9ccf0ad0","type":"text","text":"Just before liberation, some prisoners were toyed with.","x":760,"y":1063,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ad788bfd94b9152e","type":"text","text":"Months before liberation, rations significantly decreased → cannibalism ensued sometimes.","x":761,"y":1240,"width":419,"height":101},
		{"id":"543c19c002c27f24","type":"text","text":"- Born in Austria\n- Initially wanted to publish book anomously (not intended for fame or money)\n","x":560,"y":-426,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"9ca60228338f3641","type":"text","text":"**Meaning**","x":120,"y":-720,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"63ef7abc0714820f","type":"text","text":"We must have meaning in life to have hope and continue life.","x":464,"y":-815,"width":313,"height":95},
		{"id":"221f1a0b385d3244","type":"text","text":"Can discover in:\n1. Work / Deed\n2. Love\n3. Attitude towards suffering (not necessary)","x":548,"y":-660,"width":409,"height":207},
		{"id":"c03eeaf8ee665760","type":"text","text":"[[The prisoner who lost faith in the future - was doomed]]\n\n> [!QUOTE]\n>  “The prisoner who had lost faith in the future–his future–was doomed … There he remained, lying in his own excreta, and nothing bothered him more.”\n> pp.82","x":842,"y":-977,"width":876,"height":210},
		{"id":"ea2d4bf4c63012fe","type":"text","text":"[[What we need to do instead of asking what the meaning of life is]]\n>”Life ultimately means taking the responsibility to finding the right answer to its problems and to fulfill the tasks which it constantly sets for each individual.”\n>pp.85\n\nJust keep fulfilling your tasks, working towards your goals and carrying on with what matters *to you*. This is more important than finding a meaning to life.","x":686,"y":-1280,"width":1039,"height":240},
		{"id":"d1865d34400a8a4f","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “We had literally lost the ability to feel pleased and had to relearn it slowly.”\n\nPowerful extract. Imagine forgetting the feeling of happiness. *You really need to appreciate literally everything that you have.*","x":1479,"y":529,"width":801,"height":231},
		{"id":"aba476d457f3951d","type":"text","text":"[[Stoicism]]","x":-1118,"y":60,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"cde01592baa22b65","type":"text","text":"We can’t control everything (ie. Frankl going to Auschwitz) BUT we can control how we react to the situations.","x":-2058,"y":-290,"width":360,"height":100},
		{"id":"3340548d88ceb125","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “Honor thy father and thy mother … I decided to stay with my father and my mother upon the land.”\n\nFrankl knew he couldn’t control world war 2, but he could control if he fleed to America or stayed with his family.","x":-2238,"y":-122,"width":503,"height":242},
		{"id":"b6e46628934b89d6","type":"text","text":"[[Dichotomy]] of control","x":-1518,"y":-90,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0a231caf6bb0cb78","type":"text","text":"[[Never give up]]","x":-1439,"y":-225,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"74dd639e4f1d6472","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “I had no intention of losing hope and giving up. For no man knew what the future would bring, much less the next hour.”  \npp.90","x":-2163,"y":-522,"width":570,"height":170},
		{"id":"e4c592f9f993c552","type":"text","text":"[[Logotherapy]] - Therapy created by Frankl, used as a way to help discontented individuals.","x":-900,"y":-660,"width":400,"height":138},
		{"id":"a9244f844f949c23","type":"text","text":"[[noölogical]] is the spirit dimension of a human. It contains love, passion, meaning. Probably one of the most important layers","x":-1324,"y":-660,"width":348,"height":137},
		{"id":"5bc9eab951c9e4d2","type":"text","text":"**Noogenic Neurosis**\nA psychological issue originating from the noögenic dimension of a human. \nCaused by an **existential vacuum (profound sense of meaningless, lack of purpose)**","x":-2058,"y":-815,"width":512,"height":175},
		{"id":"fc3effbaa9ebeb8e","type":"text","text":"**Sunday Neurosis**\nPeople who love their work, and find meaning it their work feel sad and bored during the weekend.","x":-1778,"y":428,"width":349,"height":161},
		{"id":"1babf37e2b78d268","type":"text","text":"**Delusion of reprieve syndrome**\n\nCondemned man, immediately before execution gets an illusion that he might be reprieved at the very last minute.","x":-1501,"y":276,"width":350,"height":157},
		{"id":"89073f5f2d362f54","type":"text","text":"[[Pyschology]]","x":-820,"y":368,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ae0a99c3dd0b531d","type":"text","text":"**”Provisional existence of unknown limit”**\nA depressing fact of camp was the unknown period of imprisonment","x":-1429,"y":589,"width":374,"height":111},
		{"id":"dfbcb1edb3c54393","type":"text","text":"On paper: 300g bread and 994 mL of thin soup everyday, but it could be less, especially towards the end of the war","x":-960,"y":744,"width":339,"height":116},
		{"id":"7a8bd4bdcd8b2552","type":"text","text":"***Kapos***\n- Prisoners \n- Special privleges\n- Generally lived better than other prisoners\n- However, had to satisfy Nazis → they committed bad acts against others\n- Many enjoyed their work and brutally beat or killed weaker prisoners","x":-1375,"y":1540,"width":495,"height":260},
		{"id":"2e2457ef10f495f6","type":"text","text":"[[The Three Dimensions of a Human Being]]\n1. Somatic - Physical (Body)\n2. Psychic (Mind)\n3. Noögenic (Spirit)","x":-1698,"y":-1120,"width":420,"height":203},
		{"id":"62ea1ec9b633af58","x":-1004,"y":-1054,"width":504,"height":137,"type":"text","text":"Logotherapy aims to broaden a patient’s visual perspective of meaning. Just like how an optomertrist broadens a patient’s physical sight"},
		{"id":"6d1311711c74b4e5","x":1280,"y":-660,"width":564,"height":118,"type":"text","text":"Frankl suggests that one can find meaning by transforming suffering (tragedy) into personal triump, a direct relation of [[Dichotomy of Control]]"},
		{"id":"84ab5bdb48931450","x":-826,"y":1337,"width":385,"height":106,"type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE\n> “Survival rate was one in 28”"},
		{"id":"5b1979ea6bd200bc","x":652,"y":-1520,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"**Giving Up**"},
		{"id":"7dded230b62c91fa","x":1074,"y":-1728,"width":626,"height":208,"type":"text","text":"Page source: 141\n\nFrankl describes the horrendous reality of giving up, alluding to his past experiences. Prisoners who decided to “"}
	],
